//! #7571: a plain `--stream-errors` walk (no `-n`, no `-s`) read every event of its input into a list
//! of owned values before the program ran, about 34 times the input at its peak (350 MB for 9.7 MB of
//! NDJSON, where jq 1.7.1 stays near 2.5 MB). It now takes the route a plain `--stream` walk has
//! taken since #5498: the stream reader is seeded behind the input queue, reads its files a chunk at
//! a time, and the main loop pops each event as `input` does, a fault being an event of its own.
//!
//! The answers do not change, which the rows below pin over the new route: every program over every
//! input (clean, a fault mid-stream, a fault at the end, empty), on standard input, from a file and
//! from two files cut in the middle of the text, and with a file that does not exist or a directory
//! among the files, as jq 1.7.1 prints them. jq 1.8.2 differs on 100 rows, none about this route: 8
//! that read past the end of the input name the last file where 1.7.1 says `null` or `<unknown>`
//! (as `[inputs], error("x")` does on any route), and 92 with a directory given last print
//! `jq: error: Is a directory`, which 1.7.1 does not (`jq . a.json dir` too). 1.7.1 stays the
//! reference.

use anyhow::Result;

use crate::spawn_jq;

/// (where the input is, the program, the input, jq 1.7.1's stdout, its stderr with `FILE0`, `FILE1`,
/// `MISSING` and `DIR` for the paths, its exit status). The input is on standard input, in one file
/// (`file`), or in two files that hold its halves (`split`); a file that does not exist comes after
/// the one file (`miss-after`), before it (`miss-before`) or between the halves (`miss-between`), and
/// `dir-after` names a directory after the one file.
type Row = (
    &'static str,
    &'static str,
    &'static str,
    &'static str,
    &'static str,
    i32,
);

